{
  "_comment": [
    "Baseline of code-scanning alerts that were dismissed through the GitHub web UI.",
    "",
    "Why this file exists: security_alerts_check.py asked for state=open and",
    "reported 'OK (no open alerts in any feed)' while 294 alerts sat in the",
    "dismissed state -- 78 of them high or critical. Ivan could see them in the",
    "UI; the gate could not, because a human pressing Dismiss removes an alert",
    "from the only query the gate ran. That makes the button a silent override of",
    "the release gate: any future finding can be closed by hand and CI stays green.",
    "",
    "So the dismissals become data in the repository. Each rule id records how",
    "many alerts were dismissed against it and under which reason. The gate",
    "compares live GitHub state to this file and fails when a rule gains",
    "dismissals that were never written down, or when a rule appears that is not",
    "here at all. Dismissing an alert is still allowed -- it just has to be",
    "committed, reviewed as a diff, and carry the same weight as changing code.",
    "",
    "Shrinking numbers are fine and never fail: fixing an alert for real should",
    "not require editing this file. Run scripts/dismissed_alerts_gate.py --update",
    "to re-record the baseline after a deliberate dismissal."
  ],
